Scanner design prototypes

We came up with three new design options for the scanner casing, each building on our earlier models and on Nadia’s feedback.

Design prototype #1
This design is an improved version of our initial model. It has a more rectangular shape with heavily rounded edges,
combining elements of our original design with Nadia’s idea of referencing the look of wax cylinders. 
Since this version is already thicker, we added a handle to make it easier to hold.

Design prototype #2
This design directly imitates the appearance of the wax cylinder that inspired Nadia’s project. 
Because the components need to fit inside, the casing has to be more elongated so it looks clean and intentional rather than bulky. 
We also discussed colour. The casing could stay black, or we could print it in one of the tapestry’s colours 
so it feels like part of the art piece instead of a separate tool.

Design prototype #3
This design is also cylindrical, to replicate traditional wax cylinders.
The base is flatter because Nadia mentioned she would prefer to hold the object by its sides rather than like a flashlight.
To respect her original vision while accommodating this, we added a handle instead of adding grips to the sides.

The wax cylinder reference is meaningful for the project. Wax cylinders were one of the first ways people recorded and preserved voices, 
which connects directly to our question of how ceramic can “remember” a voice. 
Having the scanner look like one makes the object part of the story, not just the technology behind it.

From colour to lightness

We got some beads from the studio to test with, and this changed our approach. The beads are very light in colour,
so the differences between them were too subtle for the sensor to tell apart reliably by colour.
Instead, we’re now scanning for the lightness of each bead. The differences in brightness are easier to detect consistently,
which should make the scanner more reliable.

This also changes how the sound will work. The song will need to be cut into sections so each one can be matched to a different level of lightness.
When the scanner reads a bead, it will play the section that corresponds to that bead’s lightness. 
We’ll need to decide how many lightness levels the sensor can realistically tell apart, 
since that determines how many sections the song gets cut into.

Progress and status
We’re continuing to work with the Arduino for the sensor and audio.
The 3D design for the new casing isn’t finished yet.
Nadia’s ceramic piece isn’t done yet, but we’re still testing with the beads we have.
Reflection

Switching from colour to lightness felt like a setback at first, but it’s a good example of letting the material guide the design.
Nadia’s beads are subtle and soft in tone, and instead of forcing them to fit our original plan, we adapted the system to how they actually are.
It also simplifies things technically, which matters with the final deadline getting close.

Next steps
Choose one of the three designs with Nadia and finish the 3D model.
Test how many lightness levels the sensor can reliably detect.
Cut the song into sections that match those lightness levels.
Connect the lightness readings to the audio on the Arduino.
Keep checking on the progress of Nadia’s piece.
